ass="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurgen-i-of-lori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurgen I of Lori</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurgen, also known as Kiurike I, was the son of King Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia and founder of the Kingdom of Lori, also called Tashir-Dzoraget, in northern Armenia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurgen-king-of-the-iberians"><span class="enc-name">Gurgen, King of the Iberians</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurgen was a Georgian Bagratid prince of Tao-Klarjeti who held the title King of Kings of the Iberians from 994 until his death in 1008.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurgenes"><span class="enc-name">Gurgenes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurgenes was a king of Iberia in eastern Georgia in the 520s, known from the Byzantine historian Procopius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurindam-dua-belas"><span class="enc-name">Gurindam Dua Belas</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurindam Dua Belas ('Twelve Gurindam') is a Malay poem of moral and religious advice written by Raja Ali Haji on Penyengat island in 1847.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurjars"><span class="enc-name">Gurjars</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurjars are a community of north-western India and Pakistan, historically pastoralists and farmers, whose name appears in sources from the sixth century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurkhas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurkhas</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurkhas, or Gorkhas, were originally the soldiers of the Gorkha kingdom, whose king Prithvi Narayan Shah unified Nepal from 1768.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurma-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurma-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurma people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurma, or Gourmantché, are a Gur-speaking people of eastern Burkina Faso and neighbouring parts of Niger, Togo and Benin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurs-internment-camp"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurs-internment-camp.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurs internment camp</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurs was an internment camp in the foothills of the Pyrenees in south-western France, built in 1939 for refugees from the Spanish Civil War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/guru-arjan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Guru Arjan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Guru Arjan was the fifth of the ten Sikh Gurus, born in Goindval in the Punjab, the youngest son of Guru Ram Das, whom he succeeded in 1581.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/guru-gobind-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Guru Gobind Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Guru Gobind Singh was the tenth Sikh Guru, who in 1699 founded the Khalsa, the order of initiated Sikhs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/guru-granth-sahib.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Guru Granth Sahib</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Guru Granth Sahib is the central holy scripture of Sikhism, regarded by Sikhs as the final, sovereign and eternal Guru after the ten human gurus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak"><span class="enc-name">Guru Nanak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Guru Nanak was the founder of Sikhism and the first of its ten gurus, born near Lahore in 1469.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurugram"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurugram.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurugram</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurugram, known until 2016 as Gurgaon, is a city in Haryana, India, south-west of Delhi and part of the National Capital Region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurung"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gurung.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gurung</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gurung, who call themselves Tamu, are a people of the hills of central Nepal, especially around the Annapurna range in Lamjung, Kaski and Gorkha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/guruuswa"><span class="enc-name">Guruuswa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Guruuswa was a southern province of the Mutapa state on the Zimbabwean plateau.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurzil"><span class="enc-name">Gurzil</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Gurzil was a Berber god worshipped by the Laguatan and other tribes of Tripolitania in late antiquity, represented as a bull and said to be the son of the god Ammon and…</span></a></li></ul>
